<Base
  title="Treatment and management"
  description="Every path for managing pelvic organ prolapse: non-surgical options, surgery, recovery, and recurrence, explained honestly."
  current="/treatment/"
>
  <div class="narrow">
    <span class="label">Treatment</span>
    <h1>There is no single right answer</h1>
    <p>
      There is no one correct treatment for prolapse. There is the right
      treatment <em>for you</em>, at this point in your life, given your
      symptoms, your health, your plans, and what you actually want. Some women
      manage prolapse for decades without ever having surgery. Others choose
      surgery and are very glad they did. Both of those are good outcomes.
    </p>
    <p>
      These four pages lay out the options honestly, including the parts that
      usually get glossed over.
    </p>
  </div>

  <section class="band">
    <ul class="doors doors--2 wrap">
      <li>
        <a class="door" href="/treatment/non-surgical/">
          <h2>Non-surgical options</h2>
          <p>
            Pelvic floor physiotherapy, pessaries, hormones, and everyday
            habits. Genuinely effective, low risk, and never a consolation
            prize.
          </p>
          <span class="go">Read more</span>
        </a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a class="door" href="/treatment/surgery/">
          <h2>Understanding surgery</h2>
          <p>
            The two families of prolapse surgery, reconstructive and
            obliterative, and what colpocleisis really involves.
          </p>
          <span class="go">Read more</span>
        </a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a class="door" href="/treatment/recovery/">
          <h2>Recovery after surgery</h2>
          <p>
            Realistic expectations for the first weeks, the warning signs, and
            why activity restrictions vary so much between surgeons.
          </p>
          <span class="go">Read more</span>
        </a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a class="door" href="/treatment/recurrence/">
          <h2>Will it come back?</h2>
          <p>
            Recurrence explained honestly. The real numbers, what they actually
            mean, and what you can do to protect a repair.
          </p>
          <span class="go">Read more</span>
        </a>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</Base>
